## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to blend Trie prefix search with balanced BSTs for dynamic scores, plus a top-k retrieval strategy that scales efficiently. It tests practical update propagation and memory trade-offs in a high-throughput autocomplete system.

## Key Concepts
- Trie for prefix lookup
- AVL/balanced BST per node for phrase-score storage
- Top-k extraction via a front-to-back heap or subtree aggregation
- Update propagation costs and memory considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.words = new AVLTree(); // key: phrase, value: score
    this.topK = new MaxHeap(k); // top-k scores for subtree
  }
}

class AutoCompleteDictionary {
  constructor(k = 10) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.k = k;
  }

  insertPhrase(phrase, score) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of phrase) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.words.insert(phrase, score);
      node.topK.insert(score);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTopK(k);
  }
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis
- insertPhrase: O(L log n) where L is phrase length, n is phrases per prefix
- updateScore: O(L log n) for BST updates plus O(L) for heap propagation
- deletePhrase: O(L log n) for BST removal and heap cleanup
- queryTopK: O(L + k log k) for traversal plus heap extraction

## Memory Trade-offs
- Trie structure: O(N × L) where N is total phrases, L is average length
- BST per node: O(N) total across all nodes
- Top-k heaps: O(N × k) worst-case, but typically O(N) with pruning

## Edge Cases
- Duplicate phrases: update existing entry instead of inserting
- Score ties: use lexicographic ordering as tiebreaker
- Empty prefix: return global top-k from root node
- Large k: consider segment tree for better range queries

## Comparison with Alternatives
- Hash map + sorting: O(1) insert but O(n log n) prefix queries
- Suffix tree: better substring search but higher memory overhead
- Pure Trie with scores: O(L) queries but O(n) updates for score changes
- Hybrid approach (proposed): balances prefix efficiency with dynamic updates

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests your ability to combine multiple data structures to solve a real-world analytics challenge: maintaining sliding window aggregations with fast top-K retrieval under tight latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

1. **Circular bucket array** enables O(1) bucketing and automatic expiration of old events
2. **Hashmap aggregation** provides O(1) lookup and update for per-user totals across categories
3. **Max-heap with lazy deletion** avoids the overhead of rebuilding the heap on every expiration; stale entries are filtered on-demand during `topK`
4. **Lazy expiration** ensures we never scan individual events during query time—only expired buckets trigger cleanup

## Code Example

```javascript
class WindowedTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buckets = new Array(W).fill(null).map(() => ({ head: null, tail: null, ts: null }));
    this.totals = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
    this.head = 0;
  }

  insertEvent(ts, userId, category, value) {
    const idx = ts % this.W;
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];
    const event = { userId, category, value, ts, next: null };
    if (!bucket.head) bucket.head = bucket.tail = event;
    else bucket.tail = bucket.tail.next = event;
    bucket.ts = ts;

    this.totals.set(userId, (this.totals.get(userId) || 0) + value);
    this.heap.push({ total: this.totals.get(userId), userId, ts });
  }

  advanceTime(now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    while (this.buckets[this.head].ts !== null && this.buckets[this.head].ts < cutoff) {
      let event = this.buckets[this.head].head;
      while (event) {
        this.totals.set(event.userId, (this.totals.get(event.userId) || 0) - event.value);
        event = event.next;
      }
      this.buckets[this.head] = { head: null, tail: null, ts: null };
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
    }
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    this.advanceTime(now);
    const result = [];
    let i = 0;
    while (result.length < K && i < this.heap.length) {
      const entry = this.heap[i++];
      if (this.totals.get(entry.userId) === entry.total) {
        result.push(entry.userId);
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- **Per-category top-K**: Maintain separate totals per `(userId, category)` and separate heaps for each category
- **Bounded memory**: Add a periodic rebuild phase or use LRU eviction for rarely-active user_ids
- **Distributed extension**: Partition by user_id hash across nodes, aggregate partial results

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests practical DP skills: counting subsets, managing zeros, and comparing memoization vs tabulation.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting
- Top-down memoization
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and zero handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsetsTopDown(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Map());
  function dfs(i, t){
    if (t === 0) return 1;
    if (i === n) return 0;
    const key = t;
    if (memo[i].has(key)) return memo[i].get(key);
    let res = dfs(i+1, t);
    if (t >= nums[i]) res = (res + dfs(i+1, t - nums[i]));
    memo[i].set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}
function countSubsetsBottomUp(nums, target){
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const v of nums){
    for (let w = target; w >= v; w--){
      dp[w] = (dp[w] + dp[w - v]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to handle negative numbers or count modulo large M?
- How would you modify to return counts for multiple target sums simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests multi-state DP design to enforce a direction-run constraint with obstacles, requiring careful state construction and both memoized and tabular implementations.

## Key Concepts

- DP with extra dimensions for last direction and run-length
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and handling blocked cells; boundary cases when L is large or grid small

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPaths(grid, L){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length, MOD = 1000000007;
  if(grid[0][0] || grid[n-1][m-1]) return 0;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:m},()=>Array.from({length:2},()=>Array(L+1).fill(-1))));
  const endI=n-1, endJ=m-1;
  function dfs(i,j,dir,run){
    if(i===endI && j===endJ) return 1;
    const val = memo[i][j][dir][run];
    if(val!==-1) return val;
    let res = 0;
    // right
    if(j+1<m){
      if((dir===0 && run<L) || dir===1){
        res = (res + dfs(i, j+1, 0, dir===0 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    // down
    if(i+1<n){
      if((dir===1 && run<L) || dir===0){
        res = (res + dfs(i+1, j, 1, dir===1 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    memo[i][j][dir][run] = res;
    return res;
  }
  let ans = (dfs(0,0,0,1) + dfs(0,0,1,1)) % MOD;
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(n m)?
- What changes if you allow a diagonal move on occasion?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Path auto-complete is a common editor feature; it probes practical data-structure trade-offs beyond textbooks.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node children map
- Prefix search via traversal then DFS for completions
- Memory sharing of prefixes and separator handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}
class PathTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insertPath(path) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of path) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, maxResults) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (cur, built) => {
      if (results.length >= maxResults) return;
      if (cur.isEnd) results.push(prefix + built);
      for (const [ch, child] of cur.next) dfs(child, built + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, "");
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for memory with extremely large path samples (e.g., thousands of common prefixes)?
- How would you support ranking suggestions by frequency or recency without breaking O(length) per insert?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to combine topological sorting with dynamic programming for shortest paths, avoiding Dijkstra's heap in DAGs. Uses O(n+m) time and O(n+m) memory and handles unreachable nodes.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort
- Relaxation in topo order
- DAG shortest-path correctness
- Edge cases: disconnected nodes, zero-weight edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function dagShortestPaths(n, edges, s){
  const adj = Array.from({length:n},()=>[]);
  for (const [u,v,w] of edges){ adj[u].push([v,w]); }
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){ indeg[v]++; }
  const q=[];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  const topo=[];
  for(let i=0;i<q.length;i++){
    const u=q[i];
    topo.push(u);
    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      if(--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const INF=1e15;
  const dist=Array(n).fill(INF);
  dist[s]=0;
  for(const u of topo){
    if(dist[u]===INF) continue;
    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      dist[v]=Math.min(dist[v], dist[u]+w);
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify for multiple sources?
- How to handle negative weights if cycles are absent? (not in DAG)

## Why This Is Asked
This probes translating a string problem into substring subproblems and requires a clear bottom-up DP setup.

## Key Concepts
- Substring DP: dp[i][j] for i..j
- Base cases: i >= j -> 0 insertions
- Bottom-up vs memoization: space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minInsertions(s){
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i = n-2; i >= 0; i--){
    for(let j = i+1; j < n; j++){
      if(s[i] === s[j]) dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j-1];
      else dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
    }
  }
  return n ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct one palindrome achieving the minimum insertions?
- How would you adapt for streaming input or very large strings with limited memory?
